  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "JJ has two binary strings AAA and BBB, both of length NNN. He can perform the following operations on AAA: \\\\\n**1.** Pick any index iii (1≤i≤N)(1 \\le i \\le N)(1≤i≤N) and flip AiA_iAi​ (i.e. change 000 to 111 and 111 to 000) \\\\\n**2.** Reverse AAA\n\nJJ wants the number of inversions in AAA to be equal to the number of inversions in BBB by performing **at most** ⌈N2⌉\\lceil \\frac{N}{2} \\rceil⌈2N​⌉ operations. Can you help him?\n\n**Note:** The number of inversions in a binary string SSS is the number of pairs (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) such that 1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\le i \\lt j \\le N1≤i&lt;j≤N and Si&gt;SjS_i \\gt S_jSi​&gt;Sj​.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN — the length of the binary strings AAA and BBB.\nThe second line of each test case contains a binary string AAA of length NNN containing 000s and 111s only.\nThe third line of each test case contains a binary string BBB of length NNN containing 000s and 111s only.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, in the first line, output an integer MMM (0≤M≤⌈N2⌉)(0 \\le M \\le \\lceil \\frac{N}{2} \\rceil)(0≤M≤⌈2N​⌉) - the number of operations. Output the operations performed in the following MMM lines. Output:\n\n\n\n1&nbsp;i\\texttt{1 i}1&nbsp;i: for denoting operation of type 111 on index iii.\n2\\texttt{2}2: for denoting operation of type 222.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\le T \\le 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\le N \\le 10^51≤N≤105",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4\n0101\n1000\n2\n00\n11\n5\n00001\n11110\n2\n00\n10",
        "output": "1\n2\n0\n2\n2\n1 4\n1\n1 1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1: The following operation can be performed: 0101→reverse10100101 \\xrightarrow{reverse} 10100101reverse​1010\ninversions(1010)=inversions(1000)=3inversions(1010) = inversions(1000) = 3inversions(1010)=inversions(1000)=3\nTest Case 2: No operations need to be performed since the number of inversions is already the same.\ninversions(00)=inversions(11)=0inversions(00) = inversions(11) = 0inversions(00)=inversions(11)=0\nTest Case 3: The following operations can be performed: 00001→reverse10000→flip A41001000001 \\xrightarrow{reverse} 10000 \\xrightarrow{flip \\ A_4} 1001000001reverse​10000flip A4​​10010\ninversions(10010)=inversions(11110)=4inversions(10010) = inversions(11110) = 4inversions(10010)=inversions(11110)=4\nTest Case 4: The following operation can be performed: 00→flip A11000 \\xrightarrow{flip \\ A_1} 1000flip A1​​10\ninversions(10)=inversions(10)=1inversions(10) = inversions(10) = 1inversions(10)=inversions(10)=1"
      }
